1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,024
Hello.

2
00:00:01,024 --> 00:00:10,004
This is NetSentinel, my solution for DataNova problem DN-CY-01: network intrusion detection with highly imbalanced threat data.

3
00:00:10,004 --> 00:00:16,791
I will show the problem, how NetSentinel solves it, and how it performs on real network traffic.

4
00:00:17,991 --> 00:00:22,362
A network carries millions of normal connections, and attacks hide among them.

5
00:00:22,362 --> 00:00:27,292
In the real traffic I used, the rarest attack family is one flow in forty-five thousand.

6
00:00:27,292 --> 00:00:34,633
That breaks naive machine learning: a detector that calls everything normal looks eighty percent accurate and catches zero attacks.

7
00:00:35,833 --> 00:00:39,018
NetSentinel treats this as four connected problems.

8
00:00:39,018 --> 00:00:41,766
Attacks are rare, so training is rebalanced.

9
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:48,886
New attack types appear, so a second detector learns what normal traffic looks like and flags anything unfamiliar.

10
00:00:48,886 --> 00:00:53,133
Tactics change, so a drift monitor notices the shift without labels.

11
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:59,878
And analysts drown in alerts, so alerts become explained incidents, and analyst decisions retrain the model.

12
00:01:01,078 --> 00:01:04,828
Flow records, one row per connection, are cleaned and audited.

13
00:01:04,828 --> 00:01:11,843
Two detectors work side by side: a rebalanced ensemble for known attacks and a novelty detector for unfamiliar ones.

14
00:01:11,843 --> 00:01:16,743
Alerts are grouped into incidents with a priority, a reason and a first response.

15
00:01:16,743 --> 00:01:20,432
The analyst confirms or rejects them, and the model retrains.

16
00:01:20,432 --> 00:01:24,303
One command runs everything and writes a full evaluation report.

17
00:01:25,503 --> 00:01:39,564
For real traffic I used CIC-IDS2017 from the Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity: 2.8 million flows over five working days, nine attack families, and forty-five thousand to one between the largest and the smallest family.

18
00:01:39,564 --> 00:01:51,092
The data is messy, and the pipeline repairs it automatically: a twelve-hour clock without A M or P M, almost three hundred thousand empty rows, and three hundred thousand duplicates.

19
00:01:52,292 --> 00:02:01,754
On 121 thousand test flows, 99.9 percent of attack flows raise an alert, and only 0.1 percent of normal flows raise a false alarm.

20
00:02:01,754 --> 00:02:08,232
Macro F1, which weights every family equally, rises from 0.766 to 0.973 with rebalancing.

21
00:02:08,232 --> 00:02:14,200
Honestly, most of that jump comes from nine test flows of the two rarest families.

22
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:20,241
On families with enough test data, the gain is small but statistically significant.

23
00:02:21,441 --> 00:02:23,613
This is the most important slide.

24
00:02:23,613 --> 00:02:25,390
A random split gives 0.973.

25
00:02:25,390 --> 00:02:28,943
Keeping whole attack sessions on one side gives 0.888.

26
00:02:28,943 --> 00:02:40,262
Training on the past and testing on the future gives only 0.18, because every attack in this dataset happens on a single day, and the model has never seen Friday's attacks.

27
00:02:40,262 --> 00:02:43,750
A supervised detector only knows yesterday's attacks.

28
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:48,225
That is why NetSentinel adds a novelty detector and fast retraining.

29
00:02:49,425 --> 00:02:55,336
To test attacks the model has never seen, each family is hidden from training in turn.

30
00:02:55,336 --> 00:02:58,566
The classifier alone catches almost none of it.

31
00:02:58,566 --> 00:03:04,958
With the novelty detector, web attacks go from 3 to 87 percent, and DoS from 3 to 71 percent.

32
00:03:04,958 --> 00:03:10,662
On average, 5 percent becomes 33 percent, for about one percent extra false alarms.

33
00:03:10,662 --> 00:03:17,054
Port scans, brute force and botnets look normal at flow level, and I state that limit openly.

34
00:03:18,254 --> 00:03:26,290
Trained on Monday to Thursday morning, the model scores 0.998 on that period, but only 0.182 on the following days.

35
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:35,164
The drift monitor noticed this without any labels: alerted traffic shifted, and unknown anomalies rose from 0.9 to 5.4 percent.

36
00:03:35,164 --> 00:03:40,614
After retraining on ten percent of the new flows, the score recovers to 0.925.

37
00:03:41,814 --> 00:03:45,506
Detection is only useful if a person can act on it.

38
00:03:45,506 --> 00:03:58,680
The console groups 22,601 flow alerts into 483 incidents, one per attack family, shared endpoint and time window: 47 times fewer items to open, each with a priority from one to four.

39
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:07,430
Here the console scores five thousand flows held out from training: 952 alerts become 60 incidents, 14 of them top priority.

40
00:04:07,430 --> 00:04:16,381
Flows the novelty detector cannot explain get their own Unknown-Anomaly incidents, so a person reviews them instead of the model guessing a family.

41
00:04:17,581 --> 00:04:28,306
Each incident explains itself: the features furthest from normal traffic, the possible attack families with a ninety percent guarantee per family from conformal prediction, and a first response.

42
00:04:28,306 --> 00:04:32,562
One analyst decision labels the whole incident, and the model retrains on it.

43
00:04:33,762 --> 00:04:46,239
Every number can be checked: confidence intervals on every headline result, three test protocols side by side, an automatic data audit for shortcuts and leakage, and a reproducible pipeline with eighty-one automated tests.

44
00:04:47,439 --> 00:04:48,738
The limits are clear.

45
00:04:48,738 --> 00:04:57,275
Port scans and botnets look normal at flow level, the novelty detector costs about one percent false alarms, and the dataset is from 2017.

46
00:04:57,275 --> 00:05:08,039
Next, I would pair NetSentinel with payload inspection such as Zeek or Suricata, tune the alert budget to the team, and re-run on the official DataNova data with one command.

47
00:05:09,239 --> 00:05:17,066
NetSentinel finds rare attacks, flags new ones, and keeps analysts in the loop, with honest numbers on real traffic.

48
00:05:17,066 --> 00:05:20,777
The code, the full report and this video are on GitHub.

49
00:05:20,777 --> 00:05:21,452
Thank you.

